# The admin credential for the human-facing pages (/, /status, /archive), read by the
# service as $ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH. This file belongs in the target's working directory on
# the Pi as admin.env, mode 600.
#
# Don't write it by hand — the value is a salted PBKDF2 hash, not a password:
#
#   bb set-password.clj            # live instance, then restarts it for you
#   bb set-password.clj --test     # the test instance
#   bb set-password.clj --print    # just print the line, change nothing
#
# The script prompts with terminal echo off, hashes through the server's own code, writes
# the file with umask 077 and restarts the service. The plaintext password is never
# written down, never passed on a command line (an argv is visible in `ps`), and never
# sent to the Pi at all — only the hash is.
#
# It lives here rather than in the unit file because the unit *is* checked into this repo
# and overwritten on every deploy; deploy.clj never touches admin.env, so the password
# survives untouched — and never enters git. `systemctl show` also prints Environment=
# values to any local user, while EnvironmentFile= exposes only the path. The unit
# references it with `EnvironmentFile=-`, so a missing file is not a startup error: the
# server runs with the pages open, warns in the log, and shows an "auth disabled" pill on
# /status.
#
# A value that isn't a readable hash is treated as "configured but broken": the pages stay
# shut, the service stays up (so the displays keep updating), and the login page says so.
#
# systemd parses this file itself, not through a shell: one KEY=value per line, no
# `export`, no variable expansion. Changing it takes effect on the next restart.
#
# Format: pbkdf2-sha256$<iterations>$<base64 salt>$<base64 hash>

ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=pbkdf2-sha256$100000$replace$me-run-bb-set-password-dot-clj
